Implementation: ScienceDiscovery (ours) — PR follows within 24h. Do not pick up without a `pickup-ok` comment on this issue.

Written-by: ScienceDiscovery Product

## Reproduction

Environment: `main` @ `5e56ea77` when filed; live `main` is now `d72cdf8c` (four CI/docs commits on top, no change under `packages/cas`) and `packages/cas/src/versioning.ts` is still blob `679f5a61`, so every line number below holds — cut the branch from `d72cdf8c`. Also reproduces at `939acdf1` (same blob). Node v22.23.2, workspace on a filesystem whose `mtime`/`ctime` tick is ~4 ms.

1. Deterministic-ish script (from repo root; `.local/` is gitignored):

```ts
import { mkdir, rm, writeFile, open } from "node:fs/promises";
import { resolve } from "node:path";
import { VersionStore } from "../packages/cas/src/versioning.js";

const root = resolve(".local/repro-putfile");
await rm(root, { recursive: true, force: true });
await mkdir(root, { recursive: true });
const store = new VersionStore(resolve(root, "storage"));
const p = resolve(root, "f");

let stale = 0;
for (let i = 0; i < 200; i++) {
  await writeFile(p, "aaaaaaaaaaa");
  const fh = await open(p, "r+");
  try {
    const a = await store.putWorkspaceFile(Buffer.from(p), fh);
    await fh.write(Buffer.from("bbbbbbbbbbb"), 0, 11, 0);   // same-size in-place rewrite
    const b = await store.putWorkspaceFile(Buffer.from(p), fh);
    if (a.digest === b.digest) stale++;
  } finally { await fh.close(); }
}
console.log(stale);
```

Run with `node --import tsx .local/repro-putfile.ts` (or paste into a scratch file).

Observed: `in-place same-size rewrite: 190/200 putWorkspaceFile calls returned the stale ref`. Re-run on the current tree with only the byte check (read the returned ref and compare with the file): **86/200 iterations returned a ref whose bytes were still the pre-rewrite content**.

2. Existing suite symptom: `cd packages/cas && node --import tsx --test "src/**/*.test.ts"`

```
not ok 16 - unchanged workspace snapshots reuse verified bytes but mutations and CAS tampering invalidate reuse
  error: 'Missing expected rejection.'
  location: '/…/packages/cas/src/versioning.test.ts:290:3'
# pass 33
# fail 1
```

Observed in 2 of 3 consecutive full-suite runs; the same test passes alone via `--test-name-pattern "unchanged workspace snapshots"`, so it depends on whether the tamper write lands in the same timestamp tick as the cached object identity.

## Current behaviour vs expected

Current: when a workspace file is rewritten in place with the same byte length inside one filesystem `mtime`/`ctime` tick, `putWorkspaceFile` returns the previously hashed `DataRef` without re-reading the file, so `snapshotWorkspace` records the old content. The same metadata-only reuse lets a same-tick, same-size tamper of the CAS object go unnoticed (the flaky test above).

Expected: a changed source file or a tampered object invalidates reuse unconditionally — the snapshot returns a ref matching the current bytes, or throws `Object integrity failure`, deterministically and independent of timestamp granularity.

## Code evidence

`packages/cas/src/versioning.ts:112-128` (cache decision uses fstat identity only):

```ts
  /** Reuse only bytes already hashed by this store, while both file identities remain unchanged. */
  async putWorkspaceFile(path: Buffer, file: FileHandle): Promise<DataRef> {
    const key = path.toString("base64");
    const before = await file.stat({ bigint: true });
    const cached = this.workspaceFiles.get(key);
    if (cached && this.sameIdentity(before, cached.source)) {
      let object: ObjectIdentity | undefined;
      try { object = await this.objectIdentity(cached.ref); }
      catch (error) { if ((error as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code !== "ENOENT") throw error; }
      if (object && this.sameIdentity(object, cached.object)) return cached.ref;
    }
    const ref = await this.putStream("data", file.createReadStream({ autoClose: false, start: 0 }));
```

`packages/cas/src/versioning.ts:266-274` (identity = metadata, no byte check):

```ts
  private async objectIdentity(ref: ObjectRef): Promise<ObjectIdentity> {
    const info = await lstat(this.objectPath(ref), { bigint: true });
    if (!info.isFile() || info.size !== BigInt(ref.size)) throw new Error("Object integrity failure");
    return { dev: info.dev, ino: info.ino, size: info.size, mtimeNs: info.mtimeNs, ctimeNs: info.ctimeNs };
  }

  private sameIdentity(left: ObjectIdentity, right: ObjectIdentity): boolean {
    return left.dev === right.dev && left.ino === right.ino && left.size === right.size
      && left.mtimeNs === right.mtimeNs && left.ctimeNs === right.ctimeNs;
  }
```

Filesystem granularity check (same host): two same-size writes 1 ms apart return identical `mtimeNs`/`ctimeNs`; 5 ms apart differ.

## Suggested red test

A single-shot assert is flaky-red on purpose (the tick collision is ~40% per attempt, so a one-shot test passes ~60% of the time). Assert over a loop instead — red today almost surely, deterministic green after the fix:

```ts
test("a same-size in-place rewrite never reuses stale workspace bytes", async () => fixture(async (store, root) => {
  const path = resolve(root, "f");
  for (let i = 0; i < 50; i++) {
    const before = `aaaaaaaaa${i % 10}`;
    const after = `bbbbbbbbb${i % 10}`;
    await writeFile(path, before);
    const file = await open(path, "r+");
    try {
      await store.putWorkspaceFile(Buffer.from(path), file);
      await file.write(Buffer.from(after), 0, 11, 0);          // same size, in place
      const ref = await store.putWorkspaceFile(Buffer.from(path), file);
      assert.equal((await store.readData(ref)).toString(), after, `stale reuse on iteration ${i}`);
    } finally { await file.close(); }
  }
}));
```

`open` must be added to the existing `node:fs/promises` import at `packages/cas/src/versioning.test.ts:17`; `fixture` is already defined at `:21`.

Run: `cd packages/cas && node --import tsx --test --test-name-pattern "same-size in-place rewrite" src/versioning.test.ts` — red today (fails on the first iteration whose rewrite lands inside one tick; 86/200 observed), green once reuse no longer trusts `mtime`/`ctime` alone (e.g. always re-hash, or reuse only when the identity is provably the same file and a content check passes).

## Duplicate check

Searched open and closed issues/PRs: `"putWorkspaceFile"` (0), `snapshotWorkspace mtime` (0), `workspace snapshot stale` (22, none on this path), `CAS cache integrity` (3: #230/#540/#297, all closed and about commit-validation cost, not reuse correctness), `versioning.test.ts flaky` (0).
